Note ordering: seal computes entries BEFORE conditional update (as existing code does) — reads not locked, but the conditional UPDATE guarantees version unchanged since... hmm, actually existing code reads runners/penalties before the conditional UPDATE. Between read and update, another txn could commit (bump version), then sealIfOpenAtVersion returns 0 → 409. If update succeeds, no other txn committed since our version was still expected → reads are consistent with the version. But wait — could another txn have committed a change and then... no: any write bumps version. If version is still expectedVersion at UPDATE time, no write committed between. But reads happened before; a concurrent txn could have committed between our read and our update → version changed → update fails → 409. Consistent. Good, same for splits.

